{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01M92Uo2p1sgYc8bqa4TitAG","type":"tool_result","content":"4 artifacts on this board\n9f651d7e-9702-4647-9a29-4640f05e2603  request          by eval_harness   parents -  eval task tier1_al_melting: Find the melting point of aluminum at ambient pressure.\n1df3c4c4-704d-4125-b6a4-ff8ddd799e4f  candidate        by worker         parents 9f651d7e  candidate aluminum (metal, trust tier)\n8e5b30fa-903e-4db7-808e-30b3927690a7  property_result  by worker         parents 1df3c4c4  aluminum melting_point = 862 K via ASE 3 + mace-torch (cuEquivariance, float32) on NVIDIA GB10/MACE-MP-0 medium (foundation MLIP, PBE/MPtrj-trained) Solid-liquid coexistence (interface direction) method. 4x4x8 fcc supercell (512 atoms) from MACE-relaxed a0=4.039 A; solid equilibrated by anisotropic Berendsen NPT at 900 K (1.5 ps); top half melted with Langevin at 2250 K (2 ps) while bottom half fixed, then 0.5 ps at 900 K; coexistence runs at 825/900/975/1050 K with anisotropic Berendsen NPT (P=0, taut=100 fs, taup=500 fs), 7 ps each. Phase growth judged from potential-energy drift (fit t>1 ps) and final fraction of 12-coordinated atoms. Results: 825 K slope -0.0094 eV/atom/ps, CN12 fraction 0.65 (crystal grows); 900 K +0.0026, 0.19 (melts); 975 K +0.0086, 0.04 (melts); 1050 K +0.0070, 0.04 (melts). Tm bracketed in (825, 900) K; reported value = bracket midpoint, uncertainty = half-width.\nb0465de9-d270-4463-812f-cf1c6d122ec8  check            by checker        parents 8e5b30fa  literature_anchor: disagree\n\n0 open need(s)\n{\n \"artifact_id\": \"8e5b30fa-903e-4db7-808e-30b3927690a7\",\n \"artifact_type\": \"property_result\",\n \"producer_agent\": \"worker\",\n \"skill_used\": \"coolorbit.property_result\",\n \"schema_version\": \"1.0\",\n \"payload\": {\n  \"candidate\": \"aluminum\",\n  \"family\": \"metal\",\n  \"tier\": \"trust\",\n  \"property\": \"melting_point\",\n  \"value\": 862.0,\n  \"units\": \"K\",\n  \"uncertainty\": 38.0,\n  \"method\": {\n   \"engine\": \"ASE 3 + mace-torch (cuEquivariance, float32) on NVIDIA GB10\",\n   \"model\": \"MACE-MP-0 medium (foundation MLIP, PBE/MPtrj-trained)\",\n   \"protocol\": \"Solid-liquid coexistence (interface direction) method. 4x4x8 fcc supercell (512 atoms) from MACE-relaxed a0=4.039 A; solid equilibrated by anisotropic Berendsen NPT at 900 K (1.5 ps); top half melted with Langevin at 2250 K (2 ps) while bottom half fixed, then 0.5 ps at 900 K; coexistence runs at 825/900/975/1050 K with anisotropic Berendsen NPT (P=0, taut=100 fs, taup=500 fs), 7 ps each. Phase growth judged from potential-energy drift (fit t>1 ps) and final fraction of 12-coordinated atoms. Results: 825 K slope -0.0094 eV/atom/ps, CN12 fraction 0.65 (crystal grows); 900 K +0.0026, 0.19 (melts); 975 K +0.0086, 0.04 (melts); 1050 K +0.0070, 0.04 (melts). Tm bracketed in (825, 900) K; reported value = bracket midpoint, uncertainty = half-width.\",\n   \"ensemble\": \"NPT (anisotropic Berendsen), two-phase\",\n   \"timestep_fs\": 2.5,\n   \"n_atoms\": 512,\n   \"sim_length_ps\": 7.0,\n   \"seeds\": null\n  },\n  \"flags\": [],\n  \"note\": \"PREMISE: Tm is well-defined and accessible with an MLIP by two-phase coexistence (avoids the superheating of single-phase heating); 512 atoms and 7 ps are enough to see the interface move at |T-Tm| >~ 40 K but not to resolve Tm more finely. VALIDATION (same model, cell shape, protocol, run length): Cu, independent ITS-90 fixed point 1357.77 K (tier1.json, NIST): crystal grew at 1200 and 1300 K, melted at 1400 and 1500 K -> MACE-MP-0 Tm(Cu) in (1300,1400) K, consistent with experiment. RESULT: MACE-MP-0 Tm(Al) in (825, 900) K; linear interpolation of the energy-drift zero gives ~884 K, inside the bracket. The measured Al melting point is 933.45 K (NIST-JANAF, tier1.json), i.e. this model predicts a melting point roughly 35-110 K too low (~4-12%); the experimental value lies outside the simulated bracket, so the discrepancy is attributed to the potential (PBE-trained foundation model, not fit to Al liquid thermodynamics), not to sampling. For the physical answer use 933.45 K; this number is the MACE-MP-0 prediction. UNCERTAINTY (+/-38 K) covers only the temperature-grid bracketing resolution; it does NOT cover the model (DFT/MLIP) error, finite-size effects (512 atoms, ~16 A interfaces separation), Berendsen barostat artefacts, float32 precision, or stochastic interface fluctuations (one run per T). The 900 K run had only a small positive drift, so Tm(MACE) is probably in the upper part of the bracket (~870-895 K).\"\n },\n \"investigation_id\": \"\",\n \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-01T01:10:33.223422+00:00\",\n \"content_hash\": \"91085857b096803e17d9048c139584c7975c8337193e29e110f583e930df99b5\",\n \"parent_artifact_ids\": [\n  \"1df3c4c4-704d-4125-b6a4-ff8ddd799e4f\"\n ],\n \"result_quality\": \"ok\",\n \"needs\": [],\n \"summary\": \"aluminum melting_point = 862 K via ASE 3 + mace-torch (cuEquivariance, float32) on NVIDIA GB10/MACE-MP-0 medium (foundation MLIP, PBE/MPtrj-trained) Solid-liquid coexistence (interface direction) method. 4x4x8 fcc supercell (512 atoms) from MACE-relaxed a0=4.039 A; solid equilibrated by anisotropic Berendsen NPT at 900 K (1.5 ps); top half melted with Langevin at 2250 K (2 ps) while bottom half fixed, then 0.5 ps at 900 K; coexistence runs at 825/900/975/1050 K with anisotropic Berendsen NPT (P=0, taut=100 fs, taup=500 fs), 7 ps each. Phase growth judged from potential-energy drift (fit t>1 ps) and final fraction of 12-coordinated atoms. Results: 825 K slope -0.0094 eV/atom/ps, CN12 fraction 0.65 (crystal grows); 900 K +0.0026, 0.19 (melts); 975 K +0.0086, 0.04 (melts); 1050 K +0.0070, 0.04 (melts). Tm bracketed in (825, 900) K; reported value = bracket midpoint, uncertainty = half-width.\"\n}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d51e2a83-c196-42c0-b009-9cc51d67c19a","uuid":"970d6fab-ab5e-4ab7-aa08-522cd6fca3a9","timestamp":"2026-11-01T01:13: