only the temperature-grid spacing. CORRECTED ANSWER: Tm(Al, 1 bar) = 933.45 K (NIST-JANAF). The ITS-90 freezing point of Al, 933.473 K, agrees within 0.03 K (tier1.json marks the copy it saw as unofficial). The +/-0.1 K uncertainty covers JANAF rounding and the JANAF vs ITS-90 difference; the 1 bar vs 1 atm difference is negligible (dTm/dP is about 6-7 K/GPa, so about 0.001 K). PREMISE: well posed. Al has a sharp first-order melting transition at ambient pressure, and the property is measured directly to mK precision. VALIDATION: the reported number is itself the independent measured reference, so no simulation validation applies to it. The MLIP route was tested and FAILED for Al. MACE-MP-0 (same protocol that bracketed Cu at 1300-1400 K, vs 1357.77 K measured) gives Tm(Al) in (825, 900) K, about 35-110 K (4-12%) below experiment. This fits the known tendency of PBE-level models to underestimate Al's Tm. Even as a model number it is weakly constrained: one seed per T, a small 512-atom cell, and the 900 K upper bound rests on a weak drift (+0.0026 eV/atom/ps, final CN12 fraction 0.19) that a second seed could flip. A defensible MACE-MP-0 statement is Tm ~ 860-900 K, with an additional model bias of about -50 to -110 K relative to experiment. Do not use the MLIP number as the Al melting point. Getting a simulation-based value within about 20 K of experiment would need an Al-specific potential validated on liquid thermodynamics (or fine-tuning MACE on Al solid+liquid data), larger cells (>=2000 atoms), several seeds per T and finer temperature steps near Tm.\"\n}\nEOF\ncd /work && ./board post property_result @/tmp/w/r.json --parent 8e5b30fa-903e-4db7-808e-30b3927690a7"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":689,"cache_read_input_tokens":25674,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":689,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"servi